  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX2 interfacing with peripherals is standardised in detail.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The following is a list of standardised interfaces:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Display interface")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Audio interface")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Cassette interface")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- General-purpose input/output interface")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Printer interface")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The printer interface was optional on the MSX1 but is standard on the MSX2.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The disk drive interface is still an option but may be considered part of the standard specification because the MSX2 has 64K bytes of RAM.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For detailed information about the cartridge specifications, see the appendix.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.1.2 Interfacing with peripherals", "Chapter 1: MSX system overview")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter1#212-interfacing-with-peripherals", 26)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The MSX has two software environments: BASIC mode and DOS mode. BASIC mode enables easy development and execution of MSX-BASIC program and is the mode most often used by most users. A major reason why the use of personal computers has grown is that BASIC is easy to use.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The DOS mode enables various languages, utilities, and applications using MSX-DOS. Most programs in DOS can be executed on different machines. The computers automatically compensate for any differences in hardware. This allows the user to use accumulated software resources efficiently. MSX-DOS uses the same disk format as MS-DOS, which is popular on 16-bit machines. You should also note that software for CP/M, which has a great deal of applications available for 8-bit machines, can be executed only by doing file conversions.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A remarkable point is that BASIC and DOS use the same disk format in the MSX machines. This enables the sharing of resources. Both are, as shown in [[[Figure 1.8|||Chapter1#figure-18--software-hierarchy-of-msx1-and-msx2]]], on the united software environment which has BIOS (Basic I/O System) as a common basis. BDOS (Basic Disk Operating System), which is the basis of the disk operation, is also constructed on this BIOS. MSX offers the same programming environment to BASIC and DOS through common BDOS and BIOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.2 Software Overview", "Chapter 1: MSX system overview")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter1#22-software-overview", 27)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 1.8]|||img:figure_1.8]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 1.8  Software hierarchy of MSX1 and MSX2_", "Chapter 1: MSX system overview")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter1#_figure-18-software-hierarchy-of-msx1-and-msx2_", 28)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The BASIC of MSX2 has been upgraded: the new version is called MSX BASIC version 2.0. And, when using a disk system, MSX DISK-BASIC can be used, which consists of MSX BASIC version 2.0 and additional instructions for disk operations. The following sections describe these two versions of BASIC.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("CHAPTER 2 - BASIC", "Chapter 2: BASIC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter2#chapter-2---basic", 29)

