  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Index", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#index", 168)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("What kind of software is MSX-DOS? What does it offer to users? The following sections describe and introduce the features, functions, and software configurations of MSX-DOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("1.  OVERVIEW", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#1-overview", 169)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS is the disk operating system for MSX computers. It works with any version of MSX and can be operated on both the MSX1 and MSX2 without any problem. Disk operation on MSX is always done via MSX-DOS. This is also true concerning MSX DISK-BASIC, which uses BDOS calls for disk input/output. MSX-DOS and DISK-BASIC use the same disk format so that file conversion between BASIC and DOS is not necessary. This greatly increases operating efficiency and allows more effective use of file resources when MSX-DOS is used as the software development environment.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Consolidation of disk operating environment", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#consolidation-of-disk-operating-environment", 170)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS, created on the basis of MSX-DOS (ver 1.25) which is a disk operating system for 16-bit personal computers, uses the same file format as MS-DOS. It is compatible with MS-DOS at the file level so that MSX-DOS can read and write files written on MS-DOS disks. In turn MS-DOS can read and write files created by MSX-DOS. Both disk operating systems use similar commands, so users who are familiar with MSX-DOS can easily use MS-DOS when upgrading to 16-bit machines.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Compatibility with MS-DOS", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#compatibility-with-ms-dos", 171)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS has system call compatibility with CP/M and can execute most programs created on CP/M without any modification. Most CP/M applications can thus be easily used with MSX-DOS. This opens up a large library of existing software which can be run on the MSX machines.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Using CP/M applications", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#using-cpm-applications", 172)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("To use MSX-DOS, a minimum configuration of 64K bytes RAM, a CRT, one disk drive, and a disk interface ROM is required. If less than 64K bytes RAM is installed, MSX-DOS cannot be used. MSX computers can only use MSX-DOS if they have 64K bytes RAM or more. Since MSX2 computers always have 64K bytes or more of RAM, they can always run MSX-DOS. A limited disk basic is used on those machines with less than 64K bytes RAM. Disk interface ROM is always supplied with the disk drive, and, on MSX machines with an internal disk drive, it resides inside the machine. For those machines using disk cartridges, it is in the cartridge.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("System requirements", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#system-requirements", 173)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS supports up to eight disk drives. On a one-drive system, it has a 2-drive simulation feature (it uses one drive as two drives by replacing diskettes temporarily). It supports keyboard input, screen output, and printer output.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("System supported", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#system-supported", 174)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS, which has a flexible file manager that does not depend on the physical structure of the disk, supports various media and uses 3.5 inch double density disks as standard. Either a one-sided disk called 1DD or two-sided disk called 2DD is used. Each of them uses either an 8-sector track format so four kinds of media can be used. The Microsoft formats for these four types are shown below.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Media supported", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#media-supported", 175)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                            | 1DD, 9  | 2DD, 9  | 1DD, 8  | 2DD, 8  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                            | sectors | sectors | sectors | sectors |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------------------------+---------+---------+---------+---------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| media ID                   |  0F8H   |  0F9H   |  0FAH   |  0FBH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| number of sides            |    1    |    2    |    1    |    2    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| tracks per side            |   80    |   80    |   80    |   80    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| sectors per track          |    9    |    9    |    8    |    8    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| bytes per sector           |   512   |   512   |   512   |   512   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| cluster size (in sectors)  |    2    |    2    |    2    |    2    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| FAT size (in sectors)      |    2    |    3    |    1    |    2    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| number of FATs             |    2    |    2    |    2    |    2    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| number of recordable files |   112   |   112   |   112   |   112   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("**Note:** See [[[Section 3|||Chapter3#3--structure-of-disk-files]]] for the meanings of the above words.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.1  Media supported by MSX-DOS_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-31-media-supported-by-msx-dos_", 176)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS consists of the following modules: COMMAND.COM, MSXDOS.SYS, and a disk interface ROM. It resides in memory as shown in [[[Figure 3.1|||Chapter3#figure-31--msx-dos-memory-map]]] when MSX-DOS is active. COMMAND.COM and MSXDOS.SYS are disk files until MSX-DOS is booted and then read into RAM after that. Disk interface ROM includes a disk driver, DOS kernel, and DISK-BASIC interpreter.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Memory map", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#memory-map", 177)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0000H   -----------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | system scratch area |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0100H   |---------------------| ---         4000H  ---------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  ^                 |    disk driver    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  |                 |    DOS kernel     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  |                 |    DISK BASIC     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  |                 |    interpreter    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     | TPA         7FFFH  ---------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                     |  |                   disk interface ROM")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------------|  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |     COMMAND.COM     |  V")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@(0006H) |---------------------| ---")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |     MSXDOS.SYS      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |      work area      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@FFFFH   -----------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The area 00H to FFH of RAM is called the system scratch area, which is used by MSX-DOS for exchanging data with other programs. This area is important when using system calls, which are described later. The area which begins at 0100H and ends where the contents of 0006H of RAM indicates is calles the TPA (Transient Program Area). This area is accessible by the user. MSXDOS.SYS always resides at a higher address than TPA (when destroyed, the result is unpredictable), and COMMAND.COM is placed in TPA.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.1  MSX-DOS memory map_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-31-msx-dos-memory-map_", 178)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The main operation of MSX-DOS is to accept typing commands from the keyboard and execute them. In this case the program COMMAND.COM is responsible for the process from getting a string to interpreting and executing it, or accepting commands from the user interface. Programs executed by COMMAND.COM consists of internal commands, batch commands, and external commands.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Internal commands are inside COMMAND.COM and on RAM. Typing an internal acommand causes COMMAND.COM to call and execute it immediately.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For the external command, COMMAND.COM loads the routine from disk to TPA and executes it (the execution of external commands always begins at 100H). In this case COMMAND.COM frees its own area for the external command. That is, COMMAND.COM might erase itself and writes the external command onto it, when the external command is small enough and does not use the high-end of TPA, COMMAND.COM would not be destroyed. When the external command ends with " + Chr(34) + "RET" + Chr(34) + ", MSXDOS.SYS examines whether COMMAND.COM has been destroyed (by using checksum) and, if so, re-loads COMMAND.COM onto RAM and passes the control to COMMAND.COM.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Batch commands are carried out by getting command line input from a batch file instead of from the keyboard. Each step of the batch file can execute any internal command or external command. It is possible that the batch command executes another batch command, but the control will not return to the caller after the called batch command is done.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("COMMAND.COM", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#commandcom", 179)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSXDOS.SYS, core of MSX-DOS, controls disk access and communications with peripherals. These MSXDOS.SYS functions are opened as " + Chr(34) + "BDOS (Basic Disk Operating System)" + Chr(34) + " so that the user can use them. Each routine opened is called a " + Chr(34) + "system call" + Chr(34) + ", which is useful in developping software for managing the disk (see [[[section 4|||Chapter3#4--system-call-usage]]]). Each execution is, however, not done by MSXDOS.SYS itself but DOS kernel. MSXDOS.SYS is an intermediation which arranges input/output requests from COMMAND.COM or external commands and passes them to the DOS kernel.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSXDOS.SYS includes a portion called BIOS other than BDOS, as shown in [[[Figure 3.2|||Chapter3#figure-32--msxdossys]]]. BIOS, which has been prepared to be compatible with CP/M, is not normally used.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("MSXDOS.SYS", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#msxdossys", 180)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------- --+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|    BDOS    |   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|------------|   | MSXDOS.SYS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|    BIOS    |   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------- --+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.2  MSXDOS.SYS_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-32-msxdossys_", 181)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The DOS kernel is the fundamental input/output routine which resides in the disk interface ROM and executes BDOS functions of MSXDOS.SYS. Actually, any system call function can be executed using the DOS kernel. DISK-BASIC executes system calls by calling the DOS kernel directly.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("DOS kernel", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#dos-kernel", 182)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS is invoked by the following procedure:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("1. Resetting MSX causes all the slots to be examined first, and when two bytes, 41H and 42H, are written in the top of the examined slot, the slot is interpreted as connected to a certain ROM. When connected with ROM, the INIT (initialize) routine whose address is set to the header portion of ROM is carried out. In the case of the INIT routine of the disk interface ROM, the work area for the drive connected to the interface is allocated first.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("2. When all slots have been examined, FEDAH (H.STKE) is then referred to. Unless the contents of this address is C9H (unless a certain routine is set to the hook of H.STKE during INIT routine), the environment for DISK-BASIC is prepared and execution jumps to H.STKE.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("3. When the contents of H.STKE is C9H in the examination above, the cartridge with TEXT entry is searched in each slot and, if found, the environment for DISK-BASIC is prepared, and then the BASIC program at the cartridge is carried out.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("4. Then, the contents of the boot sector (logical sector #0) is transferred to C000H to C0FFH. At this time, when " + Chr(34) + "DRIVE NOT READY" + Chr(34) + " or " + Chr(34) + "READ ERROR" + Chr(34) + " occurs, or when the top of the transferred sector is neither EBH nor E9H, DISK-BASIC is invoked.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("5. The routine at C01EH is called with CY flag reset. Normally, since code " + Chr(34) + "RET NC" + Chr(34) + " is written to this address, nothing is carried and the execution returns. Any boot program written here in assembly language is invoked automatically.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("6. RAM capacity is examined (contents of RAM will not be destroyed). Less than 64K bytes causes DISK-BASIC to be invoked.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("7. The environment for MSX-DOS is prepared and C01EH is called with a CY flag set. MSXDOS.SYS is loaded from 100H, and the execution jumps to 100H. After this, MSX-DOS transfers itself to a high order address. If MSXDOS.SYS does not exist, DISK-BASIC is invoked.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("8. MSXDOS.SYS loads COMMAND.COM from 100H and jumps to its start address. COMMAND.COM  also transfers  itself to a high order address and then begins to execute. If COMMAND.COM does not exist, the message " + Chr(34) + "INSERT A DISKETTE" + Chr(34) + " appears and the execution waits for the correct diskette to be inserted in the drive.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("9. At the first boot for MSX-DOS, when a file named " + Chr(34) + "AUTOEXEC.BAT" + Chr(34) + " exists, it is carried out as a batch file. When MSX-DOS is not invoked and DISK-BASIC starts, if a BASIC program named " + Chr(34) + "AUTOEXEC.BAS" + Chr(34) + " exists, it will be carried out.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Procedure for invoking MSX-DOS", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#procedure-for-invoking-msx-dos", 183)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This section describes how to type command line input from the keyboard. This is the basis of MSX-DOS operations. Several examples of actual use and their explanations will be given for the commands used in MSX-DOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.  OPERATION", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#2-operation", 184)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When MSX-DOS is invoked, the following message appears on the screen:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Message at startup", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#message-at-startup", 185)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.3]|||img:figure_3.3]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The upper two lines show the version of MSXDOS.SYS and its copyright. The last line shows the version of COMMAND.COM.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.3  Screen at atartup_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-33-screen-at-atartup_", 186)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Then, a prompt (input request symbol) appears under the version description. The prompt for MSX-DOS consists of two characters: the default drive name plus " + Chr(34) + ">" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Prompt", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#prompt", 187)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The term " + Chr(34) + "default drive" + Chr(34) + " as the first character of the prompt is the drive to be accessed automatically when the drive name is omitted. When the default drive is A, for example, referring to a file " + Chr(34) + "BEE" + Chr(34) + " on drive " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + " needs to be typed as " + Chr(34) + "B:BEE" + Chr(34) + ". A file " + Chr(34) + "ACE" + Chr(34) + " on drive A, however, can be typed simply as " + Chr(34) + "ACE" + Chr(34) + " omitting the drive name.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     ex.1)     A>DIR B:BEE       (⟵ referring to " + Chr(34) + "BEE" + Chr(34) + " on drive B)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     ex.2)     A>DIR ACE         (⟵ referring to " + Chr(34) + "ACE" + Chr(34) + " on drive A)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Default drive", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#default-drive", 188)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When using systems with more than one drive, typing " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + " causes the default drive to be changed to B. When changing the default drive to C to H, " + Chr(34) + "C" + Chr(34) + " or the appropiate letter is needed. Specfification of a drive which does not exist causes an error.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     ex.1)     A>B:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               B>       (⟵ Default drive has been changed to B)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     ex.2)     A>K:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               Invalid Drive Specification")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               A>       (⟵ Drive K does not exist. Default drive is not changed.)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Changing default drive", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#changing-default-drive", 189)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When a prompt is displayed it indicates that MSX-DOS requests a command  to be input. By typing in a command, MSX-DOS can get an instruction.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Three forms of commands exist as shown in [[[Table 3.2|||Chapter3#table-32--three-forms-of-commands]]]. The COMMAND.COM program interprets and executes these commands. MSX-DOS operations are repeats of the actions " + Chr(34) + "give a command - make COMMAND.COM execute it" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Command input", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#command-input", 190)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| (1) Internal |  Command inside COMMAND.COM. Assembly routine on RAM.  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     command  |  Thirten commands are prepared as described later.     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|--------------+--------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| (2) External |  Assembly routine on disk. It is loaded from disk at   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     command  |  execution. Its file name has an extension " + Chr(34) + "COM" + Chr(34) + ".      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|--------------+--------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| (3) Batch    |  Text file containing one or more commands. Commands   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     command  |  are executed orderly (batch operation). File names    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|              |  have the extension " + Chr(34) + "BAT" + Chr(34) + ".                             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.2  Three forms of commands_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-32-three-forms-of-commands_", 191)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Files handled by MSX-DOS are expressed by a " + Chr(34) + "file spec" + Chr(34) + " which is described below:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(1) File spec is expressed in the form `:`.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(2) `` is a character from A to H. When specifying the default drive, it can be omitted as well as the colon " + Chr(34) + ":" + Chr(34) + " following it.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(3) `` is expressed in the form of `.`.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(4) `` is a string containing one or more (up to 8) characters. When more than 8 characters are sepcified, the ninth and subsequent characters are ignored.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(5) `` is a string containing up to 3 (including zero) characters. When more than 3 characters are specified, 4th and subsequent chartacters are ignored.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(6) `` can be omitted as well a preceding period " + Chr(34) + "." + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(7) Characters which are available in `` and `` are shown in [[[Table 3.3|||Chapter3#table-33--available-characters-for-file-name]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(8) Cases are not sensitive. Capital letters and small letters have the same meaning.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("File name convention", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#file-name-convention", 192)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| Available   |  A to Z   0 to 9   $  &  #  % (  )  -  @  ^  {  }  '  `  !            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| characters  |  characters corresponding to character codes  80H to FEH              |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| Unavailable |  ~  *  +  ,  .  /  :  ;  =  ?  [  ]                                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| characters  |  characters corresponding to character codes 00H to 20H and 7FH, FFH  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.3  Available characters for file name_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-33-available-characters-for-file-name_", 193)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Using a special character called a " + Chr(34) + "wildcard" + Chr(34) + " in the description of `` and `` of the file specification causes files with common characters to be specified. Wildcards are " + Chr(34) + "?" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "\*" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@(1)  " + Chr(34) + "?" + Chr(34) + " is a substitution for one character.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         ex)   " + Chr(34) + "TEXT" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "TEST" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "TENT" + Chr(34) + "           ⟵ " + Chr(34) + "TE?T" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               " + Chr(34) + "F1-2.COM" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "F2-6.COM" + Chr(34) + "           ⟵ " + Chr(34) + "F?-?.COM" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@(2)  " + Chr(34) + "*" + Chr(34) + " is a substitution for a string with any length.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         ex)   " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "AB" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + "                 ⟵ " + Chr(34) + "A*" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               " + Chr(34) + "files with an extension .COM" + Chr(34) + "   ⟵ " + Chr(34) + "*.COM" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               " + Chr(34) + "all files" + Chr(34) + "                      ⟵ " + Chr(34) + "*.*" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When comparing existing file names and file names with wildcards, the portion less than 8 characters of `` and the portion less than 3 characters of `` are considered to be padded with spaces (" + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34) + "). Thus, a specification " + Chr(34) + "A???.??" + Chr(34) + " is not expanded to " + Chr(34) + "ABCDE.123" + Chr(34) + " but to " + Chr(34) + "AZ.9" + Chr(34) + ", as shown in [[[Figure 3.4|||Chapter3#figure-34--wildcard-expansion]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Wildcards", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#wildcards", 194)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.4]|||img:figure_3.4]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("An asterisk (\*) is interpreted as either 8 question marks or 3 question marks (?) depending on if it is in the file name position or file extension position. For example, a file name " + Chr(34) + "A*B" + Chr(34) + " is not interpreted as " + Chr(34) + "any strings which begin with A and end with B" + Chr(34) + ". It is interpreted as " + Chr(34) + "any strings which begin with A" + Chr(34) + ", as shown below.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        A*B             (" + Chr(34) + "*" + Chr(34) + " is expanded to 8 " + Chr(34) + "?" + Chr(34) + "s)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         V")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        A????????B      (Characters after 8th are deleted)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@         V")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        A???????")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.4  Wildcard expansion_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-34-wildcard-expansion_", 195)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS does not need special commands for data input/output with peripherals. This means that it considers each objective device as a certain file (device file) and input/output actions are done by reading or writing to or from this file. This enables MSX-DOS users to treat input/output devices in the same way as files on a disk. Five devices are supported as standard by MSX-DOS as shown in [[[Table 3.4|||Chapter3#table-34--device-names]]] and are specified with proper names. For this reason, these names can not be used to specify disk files. These device names with drive specifications or extensions are also treated as simple device names.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Device name", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#device-name", 196)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@--------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| Device name |          Input/output device to be specified             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+----------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |  Reserved name for input/output expansion                |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     AUX     |  which normally has the same effect as NUL               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+----------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     CON     |  Console (keyboard for input, screen for OUTPUT)         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+----------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     LST     |  Printer (ouput only; cannot be used for input)          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+----------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     PRN     |  Printer (same as LST)                                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |                                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------+----------------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |  Special device used as a dummy when the result is not   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|     NUL     |  desired to be displayed on the screen or put in a file. |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|             |  When used for input, always EOF.                        |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@--------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.4  Device names_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-34-device-names_", 197)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A " + Chr(34) + "template" + Chr(34) + " is a character buffer area and can be used for command input. The template contains the previous command line most recently entered. It is possible to use the template for easier command entry. By taking advantage of this template feature, it is easy to execute previous commands again or to execute the command partially modified. The keys listed in [[[Table 3.6|||Chapter3#table-36--template-functions]]] are used for the template operation.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Input functions using a template", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#input-functions-using-a-template", 198)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In addition to the template operation keys, the following control keys are also available. These special key functions also support some other system calls described later.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Other special keys", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#other-special-keys", 199)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|       |                      Function                       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------+-----------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  ^C   |  stops command currently executed                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  ^S   |  pauses screen output until any key is pressed      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  ^P   |  send characters to the printer at the same time    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|       |       they appear on the secreen                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  ^N   |  resets ^P and send characters only to the secreen  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  ^J   |  feeds a line on the screen and continue input      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.5  Special key functions_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-35-special-key-functions_", 200)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|   Name   |  Keys used   |                 Functions                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| COPY1    | RIGHT, ^\    | Gets one character from the template and    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | displays it in the command line             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | Gets characters before the character to be  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| COPYUP   | SELECT, ^X   | typed next (by keyboard) from the template  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | and displays them on the command line       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | Gets all characters from the location which |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| COPYALL  | DOWN, ^_     | the template is currently referring to the  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | end of the line and displays them on the    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | command line                                |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| SKIP1    | DEL          | Skips one character of the template         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| SKIPUP   | CLS, ^L      | Skips template characters before the        |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | character to be typed next (by keyboard)    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| VOID     | UP, ESC, ^^, | Discards current line input not changing    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          | ^U, ^[       | the template                                |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | Discards one character input and returns    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| BS       | LEFT, BS,    | the location referred by the template       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          | ^H, ^]       | by one character                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | Switches insert mode/normal input mode,     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| INSERT   | INS, ^R      | in insert mode, displays keyboard input     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | on the command line with fixing the         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | location referred by the template           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------+--------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| NEWLINE  | HOME, ^K     | Transfers the contents of current command   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          |              | line to the template                        |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                         | Feeds a line on screen but continues        |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|         Return          | getting input. Transfers the contents of    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|          key            | current command line to the template        |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                         | and executes it                             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------------------+---------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|        Keys other       | Displays a character corresponding to the   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|        than above       | key on the command line and skips one       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                         | character of the template                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.6  Template functions_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-36-template-functions_", 201)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | Contents of template (" + Chr(34) + "-" + Chr(34) + "    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|  Keyboard input   | Command line display | indicates location currently |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | referred to)                 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-------------------+----------------------+------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| DIR ABCDE         | A>DIR ABCDE          | ---------                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |                              |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| RETURN            | A>                   | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | -                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| DOWN              | A>DIR ABCDE          | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |          -                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| LEFT LEFT LEFT    | A>DIR AB             | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |       -                      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| INS XYZ           | A>DIR ABXYZ          | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |       -                      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT | A>DIR ABXYZCDE       | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |          -                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| UP                | A>                   | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | -                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| DOWN              | A>DIR ABCDE          | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |          -                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| UP                | A>                   | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | -                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| XXX               | A>XXX                | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |    -                         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| DOWN              | A>XXX ABCDE          | DIR ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      |          -                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| HOME              | A>XXX ABCDE          | XXX ABCDE                    |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                      | -                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.7  Template operation examples_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-37-template-operation-examples_", 202)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When an error occurs during disk access, MSX-DOS retries sometimes. Still more errors cause MSX-DOS to display the following message and inquire what to do with them. Press one of the keys A, R, or I.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Disk errors", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#disk-errors", 203)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.5]|||img:figure_3.5]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The following error might occur other than listed above. It indicates that the pointer in FAT is pointing to a cluster which does not exist. When this error occurs, the diskette will be unusable.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Bad FAT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.5  Error display_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-35-error-display_", 204)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Internal commands are assembly language programs grouped together in COMMAND.COM. It is not necessary to read them from the disk so they are executed fast. Following are 13 internal commands. This section describes their use.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    BASIC ........... jumps to MSX DISK-BASIC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    COPY ............ copies a file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DATE ............ displays or modifies date")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DEL ............. deletes a files")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DIR ............. displays a list of files")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    FORMAT .......... formats a disk")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    MODE ............ modifies number of characters to be displayed in one line")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    PAUSE ........... pauses a batch command operation")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    REM ............. puts a comment line in a batch command")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    REN ............. renames a file name")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    TIME ............ displays or modifies time")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    TYPE ............ prints the contents of a file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    VERIFY .......... turns on/off the verify mode")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.2 Internal commands", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#22-internal-commands", 205)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `BASIC []`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Starts DISK-BASIC. This is not done by loading BASIC onto RAM but by selecting BASIC-ROM in 0000H to 7FFFH by switching the slot, so it starts immediately. When `` is specified, the corresponding BASIC program is automatically read and executed. To return to the MSX-DOS environment from BASIC, execute " + Chr(34) + "CALL SYSTEM" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("BASIC", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#basic", 206)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This command copies the contents of one file to another. Specifying parameters enables various options.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("_(1) File duplication_")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `COPY  `")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Duplicates the file specified by `` into a file specified by ``. Files having the same names cannot be created on the same disk. On different disks, specifying the same names is possible.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY ABC XYZ       ⟵ copies file " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + " and makes a file " + Chr(34) + "XYZ" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY B:ABC XYZ     ⟵ copies a file " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + " on drive B and makes a file " + Chr(34) + "XYZ" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY B:ABC C:XYZ   ⟵ copies a file " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + " on drive B and makes a file " + Chr(34) + "XYZ" + Chr(34) + " on drive C.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When copying files, either ASCII or binary mode may be selected. The " + Chr(34) + "/A" + Chr(34) + " swith specifies ASCII mode and the " + Chr(34) + "/B" + Chr(34) + " switch specifies binary mode. If no mode is specified, binary mode is selected by default (except when combining files, described in (4) below, when ASCII is the default mode). [[[Table 3.8|||Chapter3#table-38--ascii-mode-and-binary-mode]]] shows the differences between the ASCII and the binary modes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("COPY", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#copy", 207)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|           |       Read from source file       | Write to destination file |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|-----------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|ASCII mode | ignore after 1AH (file end mark)  | add one byte 1AH to end   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|Binary mode| read as long as physical file size| write without modification|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY/A ABC XYZ     ⟵ ABC to XYZ (both files are in ASCII mode)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY ABC/A XYZ/B   ⟵ reads ABC in ASCII mode and writes it to XYZ in binary mode")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("_(2) File duplication to another disk drive_")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `COPY  [:]`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Copies a file specified by `` to `` under the same file name. When `` is omitted, it is copied to the default drive. The drive name included in the `` must not be the same as the ``.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("More than one file can be copied by using wildcards in the ``. In this case, the file name is displayed on the screen each time the file is copied.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY *.COM B:      ⟵ copies any files with extension " + Chr(34) + "COM" + Chr(34) + " on default drive to drive B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY B:ABC         ⟵ copies a file ABC to default drive")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("_(3) Simultaneous duplication of many files_")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   COPY <file spec 1> <file spec 2>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ------------- -------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@              |             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@wildcard description     wildcard description")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When ``, the destination, is described using wildcards, the portions corresponding to wildcards are replaced with corresponding characters in ``. For example, when")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   COPY AB-07.021 FL?X*.V??")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("is executed, it is interpreted as shown in figure 3.6 and a file " + Chr(34) + "FL-X7.V21" + Chr(34) + " is created.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.8  ASCII mode and binary mode_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-38-ascii-mode-and-binary-mode_", 208)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.6]|||img:figure_3.6]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Using wildcards in the specification of `` enables the duplication of many files at the same time.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY *.ASM *.MAC           ⟵ makes files with extension " + Chr(34) + "MAC" + Chr(34) + " from any files with extension " + Chr(34) + "ASM" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY A*.* B:Z*.*           ⟵ Any files beginning with the character A are copied to files beginning with")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                   the character Z on drive B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("_(4) File concatenation_")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@COPY <multiple file spec> <file name>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     --------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@              |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   wildcard specification, or")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   multiple file spec connected by " + Chr(34) + "+" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When one destination file receives more than one source file, the contents of all source files are concatenated and stored to the specified destination file. When specifying more than one source file, wildcards are available, and file specs can also be copied by using the plus sign.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When files are concatenated, ASCII mode is selected by default and 1AH is considered the file end mark. Thus, concatenating binary files including data 1AH by the COPY command causes data after 1AH to be discarded. To prevent this, specify /B switch and use COPY command in binary mode.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("If more than one wildcard appears in the specification of source files, the second wildcard and after are expanded referring to original file names, as in paragraph (3) above. This permits concatenation of similar files at the same time.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY X+Y+Z XYZ             ⟵ concatenates X, Y, AND Z and stores in a file XYZ")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY *.LST ALL             ⟵ concatenates any files with extension " + Chr(34) + "LST" + Chr(34) + " and stores in a file ALL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY /B *.DAT ALL          ⟵ concatenates any " + Chr(34) + ".DAT" + Chr(34) + " files in binary mode")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY ASC/A+BIN/B AB/B      ⟵ concatenates an ASCII file ASC and a binary file BIN and stores in a file AB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>COPY *.LST+*.REF *.PRN     ⟵ concatenates files named same with extension " + Chr(34) + "LST" + Chr(34) + " and extension " + Chr(34) + "REF" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                   and makes a file with extension " + Chr(34) + "PRN" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.6  Wildcard specification of destination file_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-36-wildcard-specification-of-destination-file_", 209)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DATE [<month>-<day>-<year>]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             -     -")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             |     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             -------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   " + Chr(34) + "/" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "." + Chr(34) + " are also allowed.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Sets the date in the internal CLOCK-IC. For MSX machines without a CLOCK-IC, it is written to the specific work area. Creations or modifications of files on MSX-DOS cause this date information to be recorded for each file.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When the DATE command is executed without specifying `//`, the date currently set is displayed with a request for a new date as shown below. Pressing only the RETURN key here leaves the date unchanged.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Current date is <day of week> <month>-<day>-<year>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Enter new date:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The format of the date to be set by the DATE command has three fields: ``, ``, and ``. Each field is separated by " + Chr(34) + "-" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "/" + Chr(34) + ", or " + Chr(34) + "." + Chr(34) + ". Each field can have the following numerical values:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <year>:         1980 to 2079")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                    0 to 79 (considered as 2000 to 2079)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                    80 to 99 (considered as 1980 to 1999)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <month>:        1 to 12")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <day>:          1 to 31")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Foreign versions of MSX-DOS have different date formats: `--` or `--`.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("DATE", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#date", 210)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `DEL `, `ERASE` is also allowed")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Deletes the specified file. Wildcards can be used to specify more than one files.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Since `DEL *.*` causes all files on the diskette to be deleted, in this case, an acknowledgement is required.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    A>DEL *.*")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Are you sure (Y/N)?")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Pressing " + Chr(34) + "Y" + Chr(34) + " or " + Chr(34) + "y" + Chr(34) + " causes all files to be deleted.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("`ERASE` may be used the same way as the DEL command.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("DEL", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#del", 211)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `DIR [] [/W] [/P]`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The following information about the specified at `` is listed from the left side in one line.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <file name> <file size> <date> <time>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The fields `` and `` show when the file was created or last modified. When this information is longer than one line, items displayed near the right side are omitted.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In addition to the usual wildcards, the following abbreviations for `file spec` can be used.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Abbreviation                    Formal notation")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DIR                     =       DIR *.*")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DIR <drive>:            =       DIR <drive>:*.*")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DIR <filename>          =       DIR <filename>.*")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    DIR .<extension>        =       DIR *.<extension>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When the /W switch is specified, only ``s are padded to one line. When the /P switch is specified, the listing is stopped after each display page to wait for any key input.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>DIR                ⟵ displays information for all files on drive A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>DIR B:             ⟵ displays information for all files on drive B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>DIR TEST           ⟵ displays information for all files having <filename> " + Chr(34) + "TEST" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>DIR /W             ⟵ displays all file names of drive A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("DIR", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#dir", 212)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `FORMAT`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Formats a diskette in MSX-DOS format. In other words, directories and FAT are initialised and any files are erased. Since MSX-DOS has the same disk format as MS-DOS, the formatted diskette is also read or written by MS-DOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When executing the FORMAT command, an inquiry")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Drive name?  (A,B)      (⟵ Depends on number of drives)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("is made for the name of the drive containing a disk to be formatted. Answering " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + " or " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + " causes the menu to be displayed when a drive that can select one-sided and two-sided formats is being used. After specifying the type of format,")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Strike a key when ready")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("is displayed to wait for a key input. Pressing any key starts formatting. See the disk drive manual for the format menu.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("FORMAT", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#format", 213)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `MODE `")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Sets the number of characters to be displayed in one line on the screen. `` can have a value from 1 to 80 and the screen mode depends on that value:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@<characters per line>           Screen mode")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        1 to 32                 GRAPHIC 1 (SCREEN 1)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@       33 to 40                 TEXT 1 (SCREEN 0:WIDTH 40)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@       41 to 80                 TEXT 2 (SCREEN 0:WIDTH 80)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("MODE", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#mode", 214)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `PAUSE []`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS has a " + Chr(34) + "batch operation" + Chr(34) + " feature which automatically executes a series of commands written in a text file. During the batch operation, you may want to stop command execution temporarily. One example would be for the user to exchange disks. PAUSE can be used in such cases.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When this command is executed,")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Strike a key when ready...")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("is displayed and a key input is expected. Pressing any key other than Ctrl-C here ends the PAUSE command and proceeds to the next one. Pressing CTRL-C abandons the batch operation. Any kind of comments can follow " + Chr(34) + "PAUSE" + Chr(34) + ". This makes it possible to display the purpose of the request for the key input.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("PAUSE", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#pause", 215)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `REM []`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("REM is used to write a comment in the batch command. It does nothing as a command. A space between " + Chr(34) + "REM" + Chr(34) + " and `` is required.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("REM", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#rem", 216)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `REN  `, `RENAME` is also allowed")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("REN changes the file name specified by ``. Wildcards can be used in both `` and ``. Specifying wildcards for `` causes these wildcards to be replaced with corresponding characters of the `` (see COPY command).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Any attempt to change a file name to a name already in use will cause an error.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>REN ABC XYZ      ⟵ changes the file name " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + " to " + Chr(34) + "XYZ" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>REN B:ABC XYZ    ⟵ changes the file name " + Chr(34) + "ABC" + Chr(34) + " on drive B to " + Chr(34) + "XYZ" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>REN *.BIN *.COM  ⟵ changes any files with the extension " + Chr(34) + "BIN" + Chr(34) + " to " + Chr(34) + "COM" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("REN", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#ren", 217)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `TIME [[:[:]]]`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("TIME sets the time for the internal CLOCK-IC. Nothing happens to machines that do not have a CLOCK-IC. When a file is created on MSX-DOS, time information set here is recorded for each file.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Executing the TIME command without specifying the time causes the current time setting to be displayed as shown below. Then there is an input request for a new time. Pressing only the RETURN key does not change the time.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Current time is <hour>:<minute>:<second>:<second/100><p or a>")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    Enter new time:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The punctuation mark " + Chr(34) + ":" + Chr(34) + " separates the three TIME command fields of ``, ``, and ``. Fields after `` or `` may be omitted or considered to be 0. Each field can have the following values:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <hour>:    0 to 23")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               12A           (represents midnight)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               0A to 11A     (represents midnight to 11 o'clock in the morning)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               12P           (represents noon)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@               1P to 11P     (represents 1 o'clock to 11 o'clock in the evening)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <minute>:  0 to 59")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    <second>:  0 to 59")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("examples:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>TIME 12            ⟵ sets time to 12:00:00")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   A>TIME 1:16P         ⟵ sets time to 13:16:00")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("TIME", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#time", 218)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `TYPE `")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The command TYPE displays the contents of a file specified by ``. Using wildcards in  causes the first of the corresponding files to be displayed. This command is for ASCII files, and displaying binary files causes unreadable control characters to be sent to the screen.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("TYPE", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#type", 219)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("form: `VERIFY [ON|OFF]`")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("VERIFY sets/resets the verify mode. When the verify mode is turned ON, after data is written to the disk, it is always read to ensure that it was written correctly. This is why disk access takes longer. " + Chr(34) + "VERIFY OFF" + Chr(34) + " is set by default.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("VERIFY", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#verify", 220)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS has a batch feature that allows a series of commands listed in the order of operation to be executed automatically. The file containing this procedureis called a " + Chr(34) + "batch file" + Chr(34) + " and the series of operations defined by a batch file is called a " + Chr(34) + "batch command" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A batch file uses the extension " + Chr(34) + ".BAT" + Chr(34) + ". Typing only the file name (the extension " + Chr(34) + ".bat" + Chr(34) + " is not typed) at the command line prompt causes MSX-DOS to execute the commands in the file line by line.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For example, let us consider the following operation:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("1.  Copy all files on drive A with the extension " + Chr(34) + ".COM" + Chr(34) + " onto drive B.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("2.  List all " + Chr(34) + "COM" + Chr(34) + " files on drive B.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("3.  Delete all " + Chr(34) + "COM" + Chr(34) + " files on drive A.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This operation could be achieved by issuing the following commands to MSX-DOS:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>COPY A:*.COM B:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>DIR B:*.COM /W")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>DEL A:*.COM")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("If these three lines are combined into a batch file called " + Chr(34) + "MV.BAT" + Chr(34) + ", the command line input " + Chr(34) + "MV" + Chr(34) + " will automatically execute the operation shown above. The following list illustrates this.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>COPY CON MV.BAT  -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@COPY A:*.COM B:     | creates " + Chr(34) + "MV.BAT" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DIR B:*.COM /W      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DEL A:*.COM        -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@^Z                    Ctrl-Z + RETURN key input")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>TYPE MV.BAT      -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@COPY A:*.COM B:     | to confirm the contents of " + Chr(34) + "MV.BAT" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DIR B:*.COM /W      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DEL A:*.COM        -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>MV                  invokes the batch command " + Chr(34) + "MV" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>COPY A:*.COM B:     reads the first line automatically and executes it")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>DIR B:*.COM /W      reads the second line automatically and executes it")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@A>DEL A:*.COM         reads the third line automatically and executes it")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A batch operation may be interrupted by pressing Ctrl-C. When Ctrl-C is entered during batch operations, the request shown in [[[Figure 3.7|||Chapter3#figure-37--interrupt-of-the-batch-operation]]] is displayed on the screen.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.3  Batch Command Usage", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#23-batch-command-usage", 221)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                                 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|   Terminate batch file (Y/N)?   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                                 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Selecting " + Chr(34) + "Y" + Chr(34) + " here terminates the batch command and returns to MSX-DOS. Selecting " + Chr(34) + "N" + Chr(34) + " reads the next line of the batch file and continues the execution of the batch command.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.7  Interrupt of the batch operation_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-37-interrupt-of-the-batch-operation_", 222)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For more flexible use of the batch command, any string can be passed as parameters from the command line to the batch command. Parameters passed are referred to with the symbols " + Chr(34) + "%n" + Chr(34) + " where n is any number from 0 to 9. These " + Chr(34) + "%n" + Chr(34) + " symbols are called batch variables.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Batch variables %1, %2, ... correspond to parameters specified in the command line from left to right, and %0 is for the name of the batch command itself.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Batch variables", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#batch-variables", 223)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                                                               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| A>COPY CON TEST.BAT ......... creates a batch command         |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| REM %0 %1 %2 %3                                               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| ^Z                                                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|        1 file copied                                          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| A>TYPE TEST.BAT                                               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| REM %0 %1 %2 %3 ...... a batch command to display 3 arguments |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                                                               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| A>TEST ONE TWO THREE FOUR ...... executes the batch command,  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| A>REM TEST ONE TWO THREE         giving arguments to it       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| A>                                                            |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                                                               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@-----------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.8  Examples for batch variables usage_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-38-examples-for-batch-variables-usage_", 224)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The batch file named " + Chr(34) + "AUTOEXEC.BAT" + Chr(34) + " is used as a special autostart program at MSX-DOS startup. When MSX-DOS is invoked, COMMAND.COM examines whether AUTOEXEC.BAT exists and, if so, executes it.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("AUTOEXEC.BAT", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#autoexecbat", 225)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("External commands exist on the diskette as files with the extension " + Chr(34) + ".COM" + Chr(34) + ", and typing the external command name (except for the extension) causes the command to be executed in the following manner.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("1.  loads an external command after 100H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("2.  calls 100H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("2.4 External Commands", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#24-external-commands", 226)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Assembly language routines created to work in memory at location 100H and saved under file names with the extension " + Chr(34) + ".COM" + Chr(34) + " are called external commands and can be executed from MSX-DOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For example, consider a program to produce a control code " + Chr(34) + "0CH" + Chr(34) + " by using one-character output routine (see system calls) and clear the screen. This is an 8-byte program as shown below.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Developing external commands", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#developing-external-commands", 227)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@1E 0C           LD      E,0CH      ; E := control-code of CLS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0F 02           LD      C,02H      ; C := function No. of CONSOLE OUTPUT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@CD 05 00        CALL    0005H      ; call BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@C9              RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Writing these 8 bytes to a file named CLS.COM produces the external command " + Chr(34) + "CLS" + Chr(34) + " to clear the screen. The following sample program uses the sequential file access feature of BASIC to make this command. After this program is run, the CLS command is created on the diskette. Confirm that the command actually works after returning to MSX-DOS.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("List 3.1  Contents of CLS.COM", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#list-31-contents-of-clscom", 228)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@100 '***** This program makes " + Chr(34) + "CLS.COM" + Chr(34) + " *****")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@110 '")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@120 OPEN " + Chr(34) + "CLS.COM" + Chr(34) + " FOR OUTPUT AS #1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@130 '")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@140 FOR I=1 TO 8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@150   READ D$")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@160   PRINT #1,CHR$(VAL(" + Chr(34) + "&H" + Chr(34) + "+D$));")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@170 NEXT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@180 '")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@190 DATA 1E,0C,0E,02,CD,05,00,C9")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("List 3.2  Creating CLS.COM", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#list-32-creating-clscom", 229)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When creating an external command, there are two ways to pass arguments from the command line to the external command. First, when passing the file names to the command line as arguments, use 5CH and 6CH in the system scratch area. COMMAND.COM, which always considers the first and second parameters as file names when external commands are executed, expands them to a drive number (1 byte) + file name (8 bytes) + extension (3 bytes) and stores them in 5CH and 6CH. These are in the same format as the first 12 bytes of FCB, so setting these address as first addresses of FCB permits various operatuons.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("However, since in this method only 16 bytes differ from the starting addresses of two FCBs, either 5CH or 6CH (only) can be used as a complete FCB. Next, when passing arguments other than file names (numbers, for instance) or creating an external command handling more than three file names, COMMAND.COM stores the whole command line, which invoked the external command, except for the command line itself in the form of number of bytes (1 byte) + command line body, so it can be used by interpreting it in the external command properly. See [[[List 3.3|||Chapter3#list-33--utility-routines]]] of section 4 for an example of passing arguments using this DMA area.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Passing arguments to an external command", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#passing-arguments-to-an-external-command", 230)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Information about the structure of data on the disk and how it is controlled is important when acessing the disk using system calls. This section begins with a description about " + Chr(34) + "logical sectors" + Chr(34) + " which are the basic units for exchanging data with the disk on MSX-DOS, and proceeds to the method of handling data with " + Chr(34) + "files" + Chr(34) + " which is more familiar to programmers.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("3.  STRUCTURE OF DISK FILES", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#3-structure-of-disk-files", 231)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS can access most types of disk drives including th 3.5 inch 2DD and hard disks. For handling different media in the same way, the system call consider " + Chr(34) + "logical sector" + Chr(34) + " as the basic units of data on the disk. A logical sector is specified by numbers starting from 0.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Sectors", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#sectors", 232)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("As long as system calls are used, a sector may be considered the basic unit of data as considered above. In fact, however, data on the disk is controlled in units of " + Chr(34) + "clusters" + Chr(34) + " which consists of multiple sectors. As described later in [[[the FAT section|||Chapter3#fat-file-allocation-table]]], a cluster is specified by a serial number from 2 and the top of the data area corresponds to the location of cluster #2. For getting information about how many sectors a cluster has, use the system call [[[function 1BH (acquiring disk information)|||Chapter3#disk-information-acquisition]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Clusters", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#clusters", 233)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In a part of the directory or FCB, described later, the data location on the disk is indicated by the cluster. To use system calls to access data indicated by cluster, the relation of the correspondence between the cluster and the sector needs to be calculated. Since cluster #2 and the top sector of the data area reside in the same location, this can be done as follows:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("1. Assume the given cluster number is C.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("2. Examine the top sector of the data area (by reading DPB) and assume it is S0.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("3. Examine the number of sectors equivalent to one cluster (using [[[function 1BH|||Chapter3#disk-information-acquisition]]]) and assume it is n.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("4. Use the formula `S = S0 + (C-2) * n` to calculate sector numbers.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In MSX-DOS, sectors in the disk are divided into four areas, as shown in [[[Table 3.9|||Chapter3#table-39--disk-contents]]]. The file data body written to the disk is recorded in the " + Chr(34) + "data area" + Chr(34) + " portion. Information for handling data is written in three areas. [[[Figure 3.9|||Chapter3#figure-39--relation-of-locations-of-elements-in-the-disk]]] shows the relation of the locations of these areas. The boot sector is always in sector #0, but the top sectors (FAT, directory, and data area) differ by media, so DPB should be referred to.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Conversion from clusters to sectors", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#conversion-from-clusters-to-sectors", 234)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| boot sector | MSX-DOS startup program and information proper to the disk |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| FAT         | physical control information of data on the disk           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| directory   | control information of files on the disk                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| data area   | actual file data                                           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.9  Disk contents_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-39-disk-contents_", 235)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   +- ----------------------- ⟵ sector #0")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |     boot sector     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |---------------------| ⟵ sector #? -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |         FAT         |               | Top sectors of these data")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@whole |---------------------| ⟵ sector #?  | areas can be acquired by")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@of a  |      directory      |               | referring to DPB.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@disk  |---------------------| ⟵ sector #? -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |                     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |      data area      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |  |                     |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   +- ----------------------- ⟵ last sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.9  Relation of locations of elements in the disk_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-39-relation-of-locations-of-elements-in-the-disk_", 236)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("On MSX-DOS, the area " + Chr(34) + "DPB" + Chr(34) + " is allocated in the work area of memory for each connected drive, and information proper to each drive is recorded there. MSX-DOS can handle most types of disk drives, because the differences between media can be compensated for by the process corresponding to each drive.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Information written on DPB, which is originally on the boot sector (sector #0) of the disk, is read at MSX-DOS startup. Note that the differences between the contents of the boot sector and DPB, as shown in Figures [[[3.10|||Chapter3#figure-310--information-of-the-boot-sector]]] and [[[3.11|||Chapter3#figure-311--dpb-structure]]]. Data is arranged differently in the boot sector and the DPB.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("DPB (drive parameter block) and boot sector", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#dpb-drive-parameter-block-and-boot-sector", 237)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0B      |               |  |-- 1 sector size (in bytes)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0C      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0D      |               | ---- 1 cluster size (in sectors)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0E      |               |  |-- Number of unused sectors by MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0F      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@10      |               | ---- Number of FATs")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@11      |               |  |-- Number of directory entries (How many files can be created)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@12      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@13      |               |  |-- Number of sectors per disk")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@14      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@15      |               | ---- Media ID")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@16      |               |  |-- Size of FAT (in sectors)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@17      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@18      |               |  |-- Number of tracks per sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@19      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@1A      |               |  |-- Number of sides used (either one or two)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@1B      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@1C      |               |  |-- Number of hidden sectors")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@1D      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |               |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.10  Information of the boot sector_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-310-information-of-the-boot-sector_", 238)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        -----------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BASE -> |               | ---- drive number")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+1      |               | ---- media ID")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+2      |               |  |-- sector size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+3      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+4      |               | ---- directory mask")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+5      |               | ---- directory shift")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+6      |               | ---- cluster mask")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+7      |               | ---- cluster shift")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+8      |               |  |-- top sector of FAT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+9      |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+10     |               | ---- number of FATs")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+11     |               | ---- number of directory entries")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+12     |               |  |-- top sector of data area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+13     |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+14     |               |  |-- amount of cluster + 1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+15     |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+16     |               | ---- number of sectors required for one FAT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+17     |               |  |-- top sector of directory area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+18     |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+19     |               |  |-- FAT address in memory")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+20     |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ----------------- -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Use the system call [[[Function 1BH (disk information acquisition)|||Chapter3#disk-information-acquisition]]] to access the DPB. This system call returns the DPB address in memory and other information for each drive written on the boot sector (see [[[section 4 " + Chr(34) + "System call usage" + Chr(34) + "|||Chapter3#4--system-call-usage]]] for the detailed usage).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.11  DPB structure_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-311-dpb-structure_", 239)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In MSX-DOS, a " + Chr(34) + "cluster" + Chr(34) + " is the data unit for writing to the disk. Files larger than a cluster are written across multiple clusters. But in this case adjacent clusters are not always used. In particular, after creating and deleting files many times, clusters which are no longer used are scattered at random across the disk. When a large file is created for such cases, the file is broken down into several clusters and these clusters are stored where space is available. The linkage information is kept at the beginning so that the file can be recreated. This is the main function of the FAT.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When a bad cluster is found, FAT is also used to record that location, so access will not be made there any more. Linkage information of clusters and information concerning bad clusters is necessary for managing disk files. Without this information, the whole disk will be unusable. For this reason, more than one FAT is always prepared in case of accidental erasure.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Figure 3.2 shows an example of a FAT. The first byte is called the " + Chr(34) + "FAT ID" + Chr(34) + " which contains the value indicating the type of media (the same value as media ID in [[[Table 3.1|||Chapter3#table-31--media-supported-by-msx-dos]]]). The next two bytes contains meaningless dummy values. From the fourth byte (start address + 3), actual linkage information is recorded in an irregular format of 12 bits per cluster. Each 12-bit area containing linkage information is called a FAT entry. Note that the FAT entry begins with number 2. The number of the FAT entry is also the number of the cluster corresponding to it. Read the 12-bit linkage information recorded in the FAT entry in the way shown in [[[Figure 3.13|||Chapter3#figure-313--reading-fat]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("FAT (file allocation table)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#fat-file-allocation-table", 240)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.12]|||img:figure_3.12]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The linkage information is the value indicating the next cluster number. FFFH means that the file ends with that cluster. The example of [[[Figure 3.12|||Chapter3#figure-312--fat-example]]] shows a file of three clusters, (cluster #2 -> cluster #3 -> cluster #4), and a file of two clusters, (cluster #5 -> cluster #6). The linkage from the cluster with the smaller number is only for easy comprehension. In actual practice, numbers are not necessarily ordered.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.12  FAT example_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-312-fat-example_", 241)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.13]|||img:figure_3.13]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.13  Reading FAT_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-313-reading-fat_", 242)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The FAT as described above, relates the physical location of data on the disk and does not include information about the contents of data written there. Thus, an information resource other than FAT is required to know what kind of data is in a file. This resource is called a " + Chr(34) + "directory" + Chr(34) + ". A directory entry is composed of 32 bytes and records file names, file attributes, date created, time created, number of the top cluster of the file, and file size, as shown in [[[Figure 3.14|||Chapter3#figure-314--directory-construction]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L(Chr(34) + "File attributes" + Chr(34) + " in the directory are used for specifying the invisibility attribute in a file. Specifying " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " in the second bit from the lowest of this byte prevents files specified in the directory from being accessed by the system call (see [[[Figure 3.15|||Chapter3#figure-315--invisibility-attribute-of-the-file]]]). MS-DOS also has a file attribute byte which permits a write-prohibit attribute using another bit, but MSX-DOS does not support this feature.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The date and time are recorded so that two bytes of each are divided into three bitfields, as shown in [[[Figure 3.16|||Chapter3#figure-316--bitfield-representing-time]]] and [[[Figure 3.17|||Chapter3#figure-317--bitfield-representing-date]]]. Since only 5 bits are prepared for the " + Chr(34) + "time" + Chr(34) + " bitfield, the minimum unit for time is two seconds. The year (1980 to 2079) is specified by using 0 to 99 in 7 bits.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Directory", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#directory", 243)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Directory       ----------------- -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@header    -->   |       .       |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                        .          |-- filename (8 characters)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                        .          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             +7 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             +8 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             +9 |               |  |-- extension (3 characters)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +10 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +11 |               | ---- file attribute")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             .  |       .       |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             .          .          |-- space for compatibility with MS-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             .          .          |   (not used by MSX-DOS)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +22 |               |  |-- time created")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +23 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +24 |               |  |-- date created")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +25 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +26 |               |  |-- top cluster of the file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +27 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                |---------------| -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +28 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +29 |               |  |-- file size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +30 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@            +31 |               |  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                ----------------- -+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.14  Directory construction_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-314-directory-construction_", 244)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@           (11th byte of the directory)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | . | . | . | . | . | . | X | . |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                  |       0 : enables normal acess")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                  +----->")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                          1 : disables access")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.15  Invisibility attribute of the file_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-315-invisibility-attribute-of-the-file_", 245)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   (23rd byte of the directory)          (22nd byte of the directory)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------     ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| h4| h3| h2| h1| h0| m5| m4| m3|     | m2| m1| m0| s4| s3| s2| s1| s0|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------     ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                   |                             |                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   hour (0 to 23)          minute (0 to 59)        second /2 (0 to 29)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                                           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                     " + Chr(34) + "second" + Chr(34) + " value when multiplied by 2 --+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.16  Bitfield representing time_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-316-bitfield-representing-time_", 246)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   (25th byte of the directory)          (24th byte of the directory)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------     ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| y6| y5| y4| y3| y2| y1| y0| m3|     | m2| m1| m0| d4| d3| d2| d1| d0|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@---------------------------------     ---------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|                           |                     |                   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+---------------------------+---------------------+-------------------+")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@       year (0 to 99)           month (1 to 12)        day (1 to 31)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@             +-- corresponds to 1980 to 2079")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The place where this directory information is actually recorded is the directory area on the disk (see [[[Figure 3.9|||Chapter3#figure-39--relation-of-locations-of-elements-in-the-disk]]]). The location (top sector) is recorded in the DPB. Directory entries (locations of directory storage) are arranged every 32 bytes in the driectory area, as shown in [[[Figure 3.18|||Chapter3#figure-318--organisation-of-directory-area]]]. When a file is created, the directory is created at the lowest value of unused directory entries. Deleting a file causes E5H to be written to the first byte of the corresponding directory entry, which is empty. After all direcotry entries are exhausted, new files cannot be created even if there is a lot of unused space on the disk. The number of directory entries, that is, the number of files which can be created on one disk is also recorded in the DPB.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.17  Bitfield representing date_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-317-bitfield-representing-date_", 247)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |------ 32 bytes -------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        -------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BASE -> |      MSXDOS.SYS       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    +32 |      COMMAND.COM      |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    +64 | E5H |                 | ⟵ The directory entry whose first byte is E5H is currently unused")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    +96 |         TEST          |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |           .           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                    .           |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                    .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@+32 * n | 00H |                 | ⟵ The directory entry whose first byte is 00H has never been used")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |-----------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |                       |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.18  Organisation of directory area_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-318-organisation-of-directory-area_", 248)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Using information recorded in the directory area allows data to be treated as a " + Chr(34) + "file" + Chr(34) + ". The advantage of this method is that the data location is not represented by an absolute number such as sector number or cluster number; instead, the file can be specified with a " + Chr(34) + "name" + Chr(34) + ". The programmer need only specify the file name and the system will do all the work concerned with accessing the requested file. In other words, the programmer need not understand the details of which sectors the file occupies. In this case, FCB plays an important role for directories.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("FCB is the area for storing information needed to handle files using system calls. Handling one file requires 37 bytes of memory each, as shown in [[[Figure 3.19|||Chapter3#figure-319--organization-of-fcb]]]. Although the FCB can be located anywhere in memory, the address 005CH is normally used to utilize MSX-DOS features.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("FCB (file control block)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#fcb-file-control-block", 249)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@FCB     ------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@bytes   |  0 | drive number")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@from    |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@top     |  1 | file name")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ |      |    |      filename ..... 8 bytes")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ V      | 11 |      extension .... 3 bytes")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 12 | current block")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 13 |      number of blocks from the top of the file to the current block")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 14 | record size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 15 |      1 to 65535")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 16 | file size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |    |      1 to 4294967296")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 19 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   +--  |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 20 | date")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 21 |      same form as directory")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@  (1)   |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 22 | time")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 23 |      same form as directory")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   +--  |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 24 | device ID")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 25 | directory location")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 26 | top cluster number of the file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@  (2)   | 27 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 28 | last cluster number accessed")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 29 |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 30 | relative location from top cluster of the file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   |    | 31 |      number of clusters from top of the file to the last cluster accessed")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@   +--  |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 32 | current record")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |----|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 33 | random record")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        |    |      record order from the top of the file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        | 36 |      usually stores the last record made random access")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("**Notes:** FCB usages differ, depending on whether they use CP/M compatible system calls or additional system calls. See the decription below for details.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(1) When using version 2 of MSX-DOS, here is stored the volume-id of the disk, and should not be modified by the program.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(2) When using version 2 of MSX-DOS, here is stored internal information relative to the physical location of the file on the disk. The format of this information is different from shown in figure 3.19, and should not be modified by the program.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* drive number (00H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the disk drive containing the file.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("(0 -> default drive, 1 -> A:, 2 -> B:...)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* filename (01H to 08H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A filename can have up to 8 characters. When it has less than 8, the rest are filled in by spaces (20H).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* extension (09H to 0BH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A extension can have up to 8 characters. When it has less than 3, the rest are filled in by spaces (20H).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* current block (0CH to 0DH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the block number currently being referred to by sequential access (see function [[[14H|||Chapter3#sequential-readout]]], [[[15H|||Chapter3#sequential-writing-to-the-disk]]] in [[[section 4|||Chapter3#4--system-call-usage]]]).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* record size (0EH to 0FH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Specifies the size of data unit (record) to be read or written at one access, in bytes (see function [[[14H|||Chapter3#sequential-readout]]], [[[15H|||Chapter3#sequential-writing-to-the-disk]]], [[[21H|||Chapter3#random-reading-from-the-disk]]], [[[27H|||Chapter3#random-readout---2-random-block-access]]], [[[28H|||Chapter3#random-writing---3]]]).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* file size (10H to 13H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the size of the file in bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* date (14H to 15H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates date when a file was last written. The format is the same as the one recorded in the directory.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* time (16H to 17H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates time when a file was last written. The format is the same as the one recorded in the directory.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* device ID (18H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When a peripheral is opened as a file, the value shown in [[[Table 3.10|||Chapter3#table-310--device-id]]] is specified for this device ID field. For normal disk files, the value of this field is 40H + drive number. For example, the device ID for drive A is 40H (for future expansion, application programs should not use the ID byte).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.19  Organization of FCB_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-319-organization-of-fcb_", 250)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|    Device name     | Device ID |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|--------------------+-----------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| CON (Console)      |    0FFH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| PRN (Printer)      |    0FBH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| LST (List=Printer) |    0FCH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| AUX (Auxiliary)    |    0FEH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| NUL (Null)         |    0FDH   |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@----------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* directory location (19H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the order of the directory entries of a file in the directory area.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* top cluster (1AH to 1BH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the top cluster of the file in the disk.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* last cluster accessed (1C to 1DH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the last cluster accessed.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* relative location from top cluster of last cluster accessed (1EH to 1FH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the relative location from the top cluster of the last cluster accessed.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* current record (20H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Indicates the record currently being referred to by sequential access (see function [[[14H|||Chapter3#sequential-readout]]], [[[15H|||Chapter3#sequential-writing-to-the-disk]]]).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* random record (21H to 24H)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Specifies a record  to be accessed by random access or random block access. Specifying a value from 1 to 63 for the record size field described above causes all four bytes from 21H to 24H to be used, where only three bytes from 21H to 23H have meaning when the record size is greater than 63 (see function [[[14H|||Chapter3#sequential-readout]]], [[[15H|||Chapter3#sequential-writing-to-the-disk]]], [[[21H|||Chapter3#random-reading-from-the-disk]]], [[[22h|||Chapter3#random-writing-to-the-disk]]], [[[27H|||Chapter3#random-readout---2-random-block-access]]], [[[28H|||Chapter3#random-writing---3]]]).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.10  Device ID_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-310-device-id_", 251)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A special procedure is required to open a file when using FCB. " + Chr(34) + "Opening a file" + Chr(34) + " means, at the system call level, transforming the incomplete FCB whose file name field is only defined for the complete FCB, by using information written in the directory area. [[[Figure 3.20|||Chapter3#figure-320--beforeafter-opening-fcb]]] shows the differences between " + Chr(34) + "unopened FCB" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "opened FCB" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Opening a file", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#opening-a-file", 252)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.20]|||img:figure_3.20]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.20  Before/after opening FCB_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-320-beforeafter-opening-fcb_", 253)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When a file is opened and written to, the contents of each field of FCB, such as size, is also modified. Unless the updated FCB information is returned to the directory area, directory information and the actual contents of the file might be different at the next file access. This operation to return the updated FCB information to the directory corresponds to closing a file at the system call level.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Closing a file", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#closing-a-file", 254)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS has two system calls dealing with random access, " + Chr(34) + "RANDOM BLOCK READ" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "RANDOM BLOCK WRITE" + Chr(34) + ". With these system calls, a file can be divided into data units of any size, which can be handled by numbers, such as 0, 1, 2, ..., from the top. This data unit is called a " + Chr(34) + "record" + Chr(34) + ". Record size can be any value of more than one byte. So, treating a whole file as one record (extreme sequential access), treating data with one byte as one record (extreme random access), or treating 128 bytes as one record (the CP/M way) are all possible.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In this case, the FCB fields, " + Chr(34) + "record size" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "random record" + Chr(34) + " are used to specify the record. The value of the record size field is the number of bytes in one record. Random record fields can have any record number to be accessed (for more detailed usage, see descriptions of each system call).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random block access (file management by records)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-block-access-file-management-by-records", 255)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("[[[[Ver Figura: Figure 3.21]|||img:figure_3.21]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.21  File and record_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-321-file-and-record_", 256)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX-DOS can also access files the same way as CP/M for purposes of compatibility. One way is the sequential file which is managed by " + Chr(34) + "current record" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "current block" + Chr(34) + ". This uses a 128-byte fixed-length record as the basic unit of data. File access is always done from the top sequentially and the number of records which was accessed is counted at the current record field of FCB. The value of the current record field is reset to 0 when it reaches 128, and the carry is counted in the current block field.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Sequential access (file management by fixed-length record + current record + current block)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#sequential-access-file-management-by-fixed-length-record-current-record-current-block", 257)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("A second method included to keept compatibility with CP/M is a random access method using random record fields. It can access the record of any location but the record size is fixed at 128 bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random access (file management by fixed-length record + random record)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-access-file-management-by-fixed-length-record-random-record", 258)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The system calls are a collection of general-purpose subroutines which handle the basic input/output operations of MSX-DOS. Having these system calls gathered into BIOS in a predefined manner permits the basic functions of the MSX disk system to be easily accessed.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("There are two purposes of system calls; first, to reduce programming time by preprogramming basic functions; second, to increase portability by the fact that all programs share the same basic functions. Utilizing system calls shortens program development time and makes the developed program highly portable.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("To execute a system call, enter the defined function number in the C register of the Z80 CPU and call one of the following addresses:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@0005H ................. MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@F37DH (&HF37D) ........ MSX DISK-BASIC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("For example, when the function number is 01FH and the system call requires 00H to be set in the A register, the following assembler code can be used with MSX-DOS:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    LD      A,00H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    LD      C,01FH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@    CALL    0005H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@     .")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The CALL statement is also used in operations that return values or restore registers from memory. System calls can also be used from DISK-BASIC by using the entry address of F37DH. For this case, store the machine codes in the area allocated by the CLEAR statement and call its start address using the USR function.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("4.  SYSTEM CALL USAGE", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#4-system-call-usage", 259)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This section introduces system call usages in the following notation:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: function number")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: value needed to be set in register or memory by programmer")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: value set in register by system call")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("System call format", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#system-call-format", 260)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The function number is used to identify the system call. When using a system call, set the function number in the C register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Function:", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#function", 261)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("In this section, " + Chr(34) + "setup:" + Chr(34) + " indicates the value to be set in the named register or memory location before executing system calls.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Setup:", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#setup", 262)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The result obtained by a system call is normally set in a register or memory location. This is called output in this section and " + Chr(34) + "return value:" + Chr(34) + " indicates where and how this output is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Is important to note that when using system calls, the contents of registers other than those specified are sometimes destroyed. So, before using system calls, store the contents of registers whose value you do not want to change in an appropriate place (stack, for example) before executing system calls.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("There are forty-two MSX system calls. These are listed in [[[Table 3.11|||Chapter3#table-311--list-of-system-calls]]], and are described in this section. There are four categories:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* Peripheral I/O")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* Environment setting")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* Absolute READ/WRITE (direct access to sector)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* File access using FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Return value:", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#return-value", 263)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Function no.  |  Function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("---  |  ---")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 00H | system reset")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 01H | get one character from console (input wait, echo back, control code check)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 02H | send one character to console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 03H | get one character from auxiliary device")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 04H | send one character to auxiliary device")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 05H | send one character to printer")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 06H | get one character from console (no input wait, no echo back, no control code check) / one character output")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 07H | get one character from console (input wait, no echo back, no control code check)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 08H | get one character from console (input wait, no echo back, control code check)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 09H | send string")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0AH | get string")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0BH | check input from console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0CH | get version number")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0DH | disk reset")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0EH | select default drive")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 0FH | open file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 10H | close file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 11H | search the first file matched with wildcard")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 12H | search the second and after the second file matched wildcard")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 13H | delete file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 14H | read sequential file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 15H | write sequential file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 16H | create file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 17H | rename file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 18H | get login vector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 19H | get default drive name")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 1AH | set DMA address")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 1BH | get disk information")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 1CH-20H | no function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 21H | write random file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 22H | read random file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 23H | get file size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 24H | set random record field")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 25H | no function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 26H | write random block")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 27H | read random block")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 28H | write random file (00H is set to unused portion)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 29H | no function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2AH | get date")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2BH | set date")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2CH | get time")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2DH | set time")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2EH | set verify flag")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 2FH | read logical sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("| 30H | write logical sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("**Note**: System call function numbers are from 00H to 30H; the following seven numbers are blank: 1CH to 20H, 25H, 29H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Calling these blank function system calls do nothing except setting 00H in the A register. System calls after function 31H are undefined. Using them may cause unpredictable results (not advisable).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Table 3.11  List of System Calls_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_table-311-list-of-system-calls_", 264)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;**************************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;  List 3.3   utility.mac")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;   these routines are used in other programs")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;   GETARG, STOHEX, PUTHEX, PUTCHR, DUMP8B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;**************************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUBLIC  GETARG          Note: Five utility routines included in")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUBLIC  STOHEX                this program list will be used in")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUBLIC  PUTHEX                sample programs later.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUBLIC  PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUBLIC  DUMP8B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BDOS:   EQU     0005H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DMA:    EQU     0080H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- DE := address of arg(A)'s copy -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@GETARG: PUSH    AF              Note: Nth parameter (N is specified by")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    BC                    A register) of the command line")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    HL                    stored in default DMA area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                      (0080H to ) is loaded in memory and")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,A                   its starting address is returned in")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,DMA                DE register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,(HL)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@SKPARG: DEC     B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,NOARG")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@SKP1:   LD      A,(HL)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    TERMCHK")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,SKP1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@SKP2:   LD      A,(HL)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    TRMCHK")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      Z,SKP2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DEC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DEC     C")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,SKPARG")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@CPYARG: LD      DE,BUFMEM")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@CPY1:   LD      A,(HL)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (DE),A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    TRMCHK")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,CPY1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DEC     DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A," + Chr(34) + "$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (DE),A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,BUFMEM")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      EXIT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@NOARG:  LD      DE,BUFMEM")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A," + Chr(34) + "$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (DE),A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@EXIT:   POP     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@TRMCHK: CP      09H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     Z")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      0DH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     Z")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      " + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     Z")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      " + Chr(34) + ";" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- HL := hexadecimal value of [DE] -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@SOTHEX: PUSH    AF              Note: Hexadecimal string indicated by")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    DE                    DE register is converted into")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,0000H              two-byte integer and stored in")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    STOH1                 HL register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@STOH1:  LD      A,(DE)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        SUB     " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     C")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      10")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      C,STOH2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        SUB     " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + "-" + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     C")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      6")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET     NC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     A,10")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@STOH2:  ADD     HL,HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        OR      L")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      L,A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      STOH1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- print A-reg, in hexadecimal form (00-FF) -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@PUTHEX: PUSH    AF              Note: Contents of A register is displayed")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RR      A                     using two hexadecimal digits.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RR      A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RR      A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RR      A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHX1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@PUTHX1: PUSH    AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        AND     0FH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      10")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      C,PUTHX2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     A," + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + "-10-" + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@PUTHX2: ADD     A," + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- put character -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@PUTCHR: PUSH    AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      E,A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,02H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     AF")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- dumps 8bytes of [HL] to [HL+7] in hexa & ASCII form -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP8B: PUSH    HL              Note: Contents of eight bytes after the")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,8                   address indicated in HL register")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP1:  LD      A,(HL)                are dumped in both hexadecimal")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL                    and character codes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A," + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DJNZ    DUMP1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP2:  LD      A,(HL)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      20H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      C,DUMP3")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      7FH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,DUMP4")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP3:  LD      A," + Chr(34) + "." + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP4:  CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DJNZ    DUMP2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,0DH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,0AH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- work area -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BUFMEM: DS      256")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        END")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("List 3.3  Utility routines", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#list-33-utility-routines", 265)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The following system calls are intended for input/output operations. Some examples include console I/O (screen/keyboard), auxiliary I/O (external input/output), and printer I/O. Since subroutines such as getting information from the keyboard or controlling printers are necessary for most programs, you will find the system calls described in this section useful for general programming.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("4.1 Peripheral I/O", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#41-peripheral-io", 266)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 01H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A register ⟵ one character from console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When there is no input (no key pressed and input buffer empty), an input is wait for. Input characters are echoed back to the console. The following control character input is allowed: Ctrl-C causes program execution to be halted and a return to the MSX-DOS command level; Ctrl-P causes any sucessive input to also echoed to the printer until Ctrl-N is accepted; Ctrl-S causes the display to stop until any key is pressed.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Ctrl-C ........ system reset")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Ctrl-P ........ printer echo")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Ctrl-N ........ halt printer echo")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@Ctrl-S ........ pause display")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Console input", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#console-input", 267)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 02H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: E register ⟵ character code to be sent out")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call displays the character specified by the E register on the screen. It also checks the four control characters, listed above.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Console output", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#console-output", 268)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 03H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A register ⟵ one character from AUX device")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call checks four control characters.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("External input", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#external-input", 269)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 04H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: E register ⟵ character code to send to AUX device")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call checks four control characters.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("External output", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#external-output", 270)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 05H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: A register ⟵ one character from console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: ```")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call does not echo back. It treats control characters in the same way as [[[function 01H|||Chapter3#console-input]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Printer output", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#printer-output", 271)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 06H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: E register ⟵ character code to be send to the console. When 0FFH is specified, the character will be input from the console.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: When the E register is set to 0FFH (input), the result of input is in the A register. The value set in the A register is the character code of the key, if it was pressed; otherwise, the value is 00H. When the E register is set to a value other than 0FFH (output), there is no return value.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call does not support control characters and does not echo back input. This system call checks four control characters.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Direct console input/output", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#direct-console-inputoutput", 272)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 07H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A register ⟵ one character from console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call does not support control characters, nor echo back.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Direct console input - 1", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#direct-console-input---1", 273)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 08H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A register ⟵ one character from console")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call does not echo back. It treats control characters in the same way as [[[function 01H|||Chapter3#console-input]]].")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Direct console input - 2", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#direct-console-input---2", 274)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 09H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of string, prepared on memory, to be sent to the console.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("24H (" + Chr(34) + "$" + Chr(34) + ") is appended to the end of the string as the end symbol. This system call checks and performs four control character functions, as listed previously.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("String output", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#string-output", 275)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0AH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: The address of memory where the maximum number of input characters (1 to 0FFH) is set should be set in the DE register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: Number of characters actually sent from console is set in the address, one added to the address indicated by the DE register; string sent from console is set in the area from the address, two added to the address indicated by the DE register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Return key input is considered as the end of console input. However, when the number of input characters exceeds the specified number of characters (contents indicated by DE register, 1 to 255), characters within the specified number of characters will be treated as an input string and set in memory, and the operation ends. The rest of characters including the return key are ignored. Editing with the template is available to string input using this system call. This system call checks and performs four control character function, as listed previously.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("String input", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#string-input", 276)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0BH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 0FFH is set in the A register when the keyboard is being pressed; otherwise, 00H is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call checks and performs four control character function, as listed previously.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Console status check", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#console-status-check", 277)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The following system calls set the MSX system environment; for example, changing the default drive, or setting various default values of the system.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("4.2 Environment Setting and Readout", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#42-environment-setting-and-readout", 278)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 00H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When this is called in MSX-DOS, the system is reset by jumping to 0000H. When MSX DISK-BASIC call this, it is " + Chr(34) + "warm started" + Chr(34) + ". That is, it returns to BASIC command level without destroying programs currently loaded.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("System reset", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#system-reset", 279)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0CH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: HL register ⟵ 0022H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call is for acquiring various CP/M version numbers, on MSX-DOS, however, 0022H is always returned.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Version number acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#version-number-acquisition", 280)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0DH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("If there is a sector which has been changed but not written to the disk, this system call writes it to the disk, then it sets the default drive to drive A and sets DMA to 0080H.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Disk reset", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#disk-reset", 281)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0EH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: E register ⟵ default drive number (A = 00H, B = 01H, ...)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Disk access by the system calls are made to the drive indicated by the default drive number, unless otherwise specified. Note that, when the drive number, which is set in the FCB specified upon calling the system call, is other than 00H, the default drive setting made by this system call is ignored.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Default drive setting", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#default-drive-setting", 282)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 18H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: HL register ⟵ online drive information")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The online drive is the drive connected to MSX normally when the disk system is booted up. Executing this system call causes each drive to be examined whether it is online, and the result is returned in the HL register as shown in [[[Figure 3.22|||Chapter3#figure-322--login-vector]]]. When the bit is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ", the corresponding drive is online; otherwise it is not.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Login vector acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#login-vector-acquisition", 283)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| register name  |            H             |            L             |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------------+--------------------------+--------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|   bit number   |  7  6  5  4  3  2  1  0  |  7  6  5  4  3  2  1  0  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------------+--------------------------+--------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|   drive name   |  meaningless on MSX-DOS  |  H: G: F: E: D: C: B: A: |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@|----------------+-----------------------------------------------------|")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@| online/offline |  1 is set for online and 0 for offline in each bit  |")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_Figure 3.22  Login vector_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_figure-322-login-vector_", 284)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 19H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A register ⟵ defaut drive number (A = 00H, B = 01H, ...)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Default drive acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#default-drive-acquisition", 285)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 1AH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ address setting to be transferred to (DMA address)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Though DMA address is initialized to 0080H at system reset, it can be reset to any address by using this system call.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Setting of address to be transferred to", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#setting-of-address-to-be-transferred-to", 286)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 1BH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: E register ⟵ number of the objective drive (default drive = 00H, A = 01H, B = 02H, ...)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * A register ⟵ number of logical sectors per one cluster (FFH if E register is set inappropriate)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * BC register ⟵ logical sector size")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ amount of clusters")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * IX register ⟵ DPB starting address")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * IY register ⟵ FAT starting address on memory")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call gets the information about the disk in the specified drive. Specifying 00H for the drive number specifies the default drive. For other than that, specify 01H for drive A, 02H for drive B, and so on.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Disk information acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#disk-information-acquisition", 287)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2AH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * HL register ⟵ year")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * D register ⟵ month")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ day of month")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * A register ⟵ day of week")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Date acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#date-acquisition", 288)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2BH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * HL register ⟵ year")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * D register ⟵ month")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ day of month")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: A indicates whether the system call has done succesfully. If successful, the A register is set to 00H; otherwise, 0FFH.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Date setting", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#date-setting", 289)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2CH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * H register ⟵ hour")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * L register ⟵ minute")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * D register ⟵ second")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ 1/100 second")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Time acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#time-acquisition", 290)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2DH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * H register ⟵ hour")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * L register ⟵ minute")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * D register ⟵ second")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ 1/100 second")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: If successful, the A register is set to 00H; otherwise, 0FFH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Time setting", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#time-setting", 291)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2EH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ 00H, when resetting verify flag")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * E register ⟵ value other than 00H, when setting the verify flag")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Setting the verify flag causes successive writing to the disk to be done in mode " + Chr(34) + "verify on" + Chr(34) + ". That is, by reading the contents written on the disk, the check is made to compare them with the contents to be written.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Verify flag setting", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#verify-flag-setting", 292)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("MSX manages the disk with the " + Chr(34) + "logical sector" + Chr(34) + " as a minimum unit. The logical sector is defined independent of the physical secotrs of the disk, and is numbered from 0 to the maximum logical sector (maximum number depends on the kind of the disks).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Logical sectors enable users of MSX-DOS or MSX DISK-BASIC to access the disk without being concerned about the number of physical sectors per track, where that number depends on the media type of the disk. In fact, by utilizing system calls which use FCB (file control block), the file can be easily handled in detail even without considering logical sectors, so the user does not even need to use logical sectors. But for some purposes, access using logical sectors is desirable, so MSX-DOS ans MSX DISK-BASIC offer system calls which can access logical sectors.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This section describes the system calls which access the disk by use of logical sectors.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("4.3 Absolute READ/WRITE (direct access to sectors)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#43-absolute-readwrite-direct-access-to-sectors", 293)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 2FH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: The logical sector number to be read from (for more than one logical sector, the starting logical sector number) should be set in the DE register. The number of logical sectors to be read should be set in the H register, and the drive number (00H for drive A, 01H for drive B, and so on. The same follows for function 30H below) to be used to read should be set in the L register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: The contents read are set in the DMA buffer.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call reads out a specified number of continuous logical sectors from the specified logical sector of the specified drive and stores the contents in memory after DMA. It then stores the contents of what it has read in memory after DMA. [[[Function 1AH|||Chapter3#setting-of-address-to-be-transferred-to]]] (which specifies the address it is to be transferred to) assures that there is enough available space in memory.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Reading from the disk using logical sectors", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#reading-from-the-disk-using-logical-sectors", 294)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 30H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: Contents to be written should be set in memory area after the address indicated by DMA. The logical sector number from where the writing begins should be set in the DE register. The number of logical sectors to be written should be set in the H register. The drive number to be written to should be set in the L register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Writing to the disk using logocal sectors", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#writing-to-the-disk-using-logocal-sectors", 295)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;*********************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;   List 3.4    cluster dump")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;               this program must link List 3.3")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;*********************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   GETARG          Note: The first 128 bytes of an arbitrary")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   STOHEX                cluster specified in the command")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   PUTHEX                line are dumped.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   DUMP8B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BDOS    EQU     0005H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- program start -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    GETARG          ;[DE] := 1st argument of command line")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    STOHEX          ;HL := evaluate [DE] as hexadecimal")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                ;      this is the target cluster No.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      E,00H           ;requests the default drive")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,1BH           ;get disk information")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CP      0FFH            ;fail ?")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      NZ,L2           ;if not fail, A := sector/cluster and goto L2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,ERMSG1       ;[DE] := 'Cannot get Disk information'")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,09H           ;string putput function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET                     ;error return")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@L2:     LD      E,(IX+12)       ;DE := 1st sector of data area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      D,(IX+13)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DEC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DEC     HL              ;HL := Cluster No. - 2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,L             ;BC := Cluster No. - 2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@LOOP:   DEC     A               ;Count N times")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      Z,RESULT")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      LOOP")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@RESULT: ADD     HL,DE           ;HL := sector of target cluster")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    HL              ;save target sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,NEWDMA       ;we reserved 1024 bytes area for DMA")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,1AH           ;Set DMA address function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,19H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS            ;default drive ?")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      L,A")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     DE              ;DE := target sector")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      H,1             ;H := 1 (read 1 sector only)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,2FH           ;absolute read function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS            ;data will be set into DMA")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP:   LD      HL,NEWDMA       ;HL := DMA address")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,0000H        ;DE := relative address from cluster top")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,16            ;dump 16 lines")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DLOOP:  PUSH    BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,D")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,E")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A," + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        PUSH    HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EX      DE,HL           ;DE := DE+8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    DUMP8B          ;8 bytes dump subroutine (in another file)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        POP     BC")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DJNZ    DLOOP")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET                     ;all work have done.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- work area -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@NEWDMA: DS      1024            ;Private DMA area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ADRS:   DS      2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ERMSG1: DB      " + Chr(34) + "Cannot get Disk information.$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ERMSG2: DB      " + Chr(34) + "Cannot read that cluster.$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        END")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_List 3.4  Cluster dump_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_list-34-cluster-dump_", 296)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Since accessing a file is difficult when using the system calls described in the previous section (which read and write logical sectors directly), system calls using FCB are needed to easier access the disk by specifying files.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("There are three categories of system calls using FCB. First is sequential file access and second is random file access, both are offered to maintain CP/M compatibility. The third is what gives MSX-DOS its power: random block access. This method is not available in CP/M. Random block access has the following features:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* Any record size can be specified")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* Random access can be made to multiple records")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* File size can be controlled in bytes")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This section describes system calls for file access using FCB, including random block access. Note that the following three functions do not work correctly when FCB is in the address range 4000H to 7FFFH:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("1. [[[Function call 11H|||Chapter3#file-search---1]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("2. [[[Function call 12H|||Chapter3#file-search---2]]]")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("3. Input/output for devices (CON, PRN, NUL, AUX)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("4.4 File Access Using FCB", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#44-file-access-using-fcb", 297)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 0FH")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of FCB which is not opened")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when a file is opened succeessfully; otherwise 0FFH is set. When a file is opened successfully, each field of the FCB is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When 00H is specified for a drive number, the default drive set by [[[function 0EH (default drive setting)|||Chapter3#default-drive-setting]]] is used. To open a file on another drive, specify 01H for drive A, 02H for drive B and so on.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("When a file is opened by this system call, all FCB fields except record size, current block, current record, and random record are set using information from the directory area on the disk. Fields which are not set should be set by the user after executing this system call, if needed. The state that each field of FCB is set is " + Chr(34) + "the state that file is opened" + Chr(34) + " when using system calls using FCB, and, in this case, system calls which access the file using FCB, described below, can be used.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Opening files", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#opening-files", 298)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 10H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when file is closed scuccessfully; otherwise, 0FFH is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("By writing the current contents of FCB in memory to the corresponding directory area on the disk, file information can be kept current. When the file is only read, it does not need to be closed by using this system call.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Closing files", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#closing-files", 299)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 11H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of FCB which is not opened")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when the file is found; otherwise 0FFH is set. When the file is found, the directory entry (32 bytes) of the file on the disk is set in the area indicated by DMA, and FCB drive number is set (thus, 33 bytes are used).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Wildcard characters can be used in the name of the file. For example, a specification " + Chr(34) + "????????.c" + Chr(34) + " causes any file name with an extension of " + Chr(34) + "c" + Chr(34) + " to be searched for, and the directory information of the file first found is written in after DMA. To find all matching files or to see whether there is only one matching file, use [[[function 12H|||Chapter3#file-search---2]]] described below.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("File search - 1", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#file-search---1", 300)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 12H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: none")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when the file is found; otherwise 0FFH is set. When the file is found, the directory entry (32 bytes) of the file on the disk is set in the area indicated by DMA, and the FCB drive number is set (thus, 33 bytes are used).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call should be used to search for multiple files meeting the file name specification by wildcard characters in [[[function 11H|||Chapter3#file-search---1]]]. So this function should not be used by itself.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call allows the directory information of files meeting the specifications in [[[function 11H|||Chapter3#file-search---1]]] to be listed in order, one by one.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("File search - 2", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#file-search---2", 301)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 13H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of FCB which is not opened")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when file is successfully deleted., otherwise 0FFH is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Using wildcard characters for the file name may cause more than one file to be deleted. Exercise caution when using wildcards to delete files.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Deleting files", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#deleting-files", 302)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 14H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current block ⟵ starting block for readout")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current record ⟵ starting record for readout")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when readout is successful; otherwise 01H is set. When successful, one record which has been read is set in the area indicated by DMA.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The FCB current block and record will be updated automatically after the readout. That is, in successive readouts, the current block and record do not need to be set. The record size for readout is fixed at 128 bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Sequential readout", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#sequential-readout", 303)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 15H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current block ⟵ starting block for writing")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current record ⟵ starting record for writing")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * 128 bytes starting from DMA ⟵ data to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when writing is successful; otherwise 01H is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The FCB current block and record will be updated automatically after the readout.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Sequential writing to the disk", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#sequential-writing-to-the-disk", 304)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 16H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of FCB which is not opened")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when the file is created successfully; otherwise 0FFH is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The record size, current block and record, and the random record of the FCB should be set after executing this system call.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Creating files", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#creating-files", 305)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 17H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: New file name should be set within 11 bytes after the 18th byte of the FCB (2nd byte of file size field of FCB = 16 bytes after old file name) corresponding to old file name (that is, it should be set in 18th to 28th byte), the FCB address should be set in the DE register.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when the file name is renamed successfully; otherwise 0FFH is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Wildcard characters can be used for both the new and old file names. For example, specifying " + Chr(34) + "????????.o" + Chr(34) + " for the old file name and " + Chr(34) + "????????.obj" + Chr(34) + " for the new file name causes the extension of all files having " + Chr(34) + ".o" + Chr(34) + " to be changed to " + Chr(34) + ".obj" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Renaming files", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#renaming-files", 306)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 21H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * random record in FCB ⟵ record for readout")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when readout is successful; otherwise 01H is set. When successful, the contents of one record which has been read are set in the area indicated by DMA.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The lenght of the record is fixed to 128 bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random reading from the disk", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-reading-from-the-disk", 307)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 22H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * random record in FCB ⟵ record to be written to")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * 128 bytes starting from DMA ⟵ data to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when writing is successful; otherwise 01H is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The lenght of the record is fixed to 128 bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random writing to the disk", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-writing-to-the-disk", 308)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 23H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**: DE register ⟵ starting address of FCB which is not opened")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when the function is successful; otherwise 00H is set. When successful, the size of the specified file is set in increments of 128 bytes, in the first three bytes of the random record field.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The file size is calculated in increments of 128 bytes. That is, 2 would be set for files ranging in size from 129 bytes to 256 bytes. Thus a file with 257 bytes would return a value of 3.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("File size acquisition", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#file-size-acquisition", 309)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 24H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current block ⟵ objective block")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB current record ⟵ objective record")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: Current record position, calculated from the current block and record fields of specified FCB, is set in the random record field.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random record field setting", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-record-field-setting", 310)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 26H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ the starting address from the FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB record size ⟵ size of record to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB random record ⟵ the record ID number")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * HL register ⟵ the number of records to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DMA memory buffer ⟵ the data to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when writing is successful; otherwise 01H is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("After writing to the disk, the value of the random record field is automatically updated and points to the next record. The size of one record can be set to any value from 1 byte to 65535 bytes by setting the desired value in the FCB record size field. When 0 records are to be written, the file lenght is calculated at the record size multiplied by the record number. The rest is discarded.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random writing to the disk - 2 (random block access)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-writing-to-the-disk---2-random-block-access", 311)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 27H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB record size ⟵ record size to be read")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB random record ⟵ record to start reading")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * HL register ⟵ number of records to be read")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when data is read successfully; otherwise 01H is read. The number of records actually read is set back in the HL register. When this number is almost one, the data which has been read is set in the area indicated by DMA.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("After readout, the random record field is automatically updated. After executing this system call, the total number of records actually read is set in the HL register. That is, if the end of file is reached before the specified number of records have been read, the actual number of records read will be returned in the HL register.    ")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call has been created for MSX-DOS and is not compatible with CP/M.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random readout - 2 (random block access)", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-readout---2-random-block-access", 312)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Function**: 28H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Setup**:")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * FCB random record ⟵ record to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("  * 128 bytes in DMA buffer ⟵ data to be written")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("* **Return value**: 00H is set in the A register when writing is successful; otherwise, 01H is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The lenght of records is fixed at 128 bytes.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("This system call is the same as 22H except for one point. When the file becomes large, 00H is written to the added records coming before the specified record.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Random writing - 3", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#random-writing---3", 313)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;********************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;   List 3.5    file dump")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;               this program must link List 3.3")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;********************************************************")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   GETARG          Note: gets the dump list of the file")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   STOHEX                specified at the command line")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        EXTRN   DUMP8B")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@BDOS:   EQU     0005H           Note: The file name specified as the first")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@FCB:    EQU     005CH                 parameter of the command line is")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                      stored in the default FCB area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@                                      from (005CH)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- program start -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,FCB          ;DE := default FCB address")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,0FH           ;open file function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        OR      A               ;success ?")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      Z,READ          ;if so, goto READ")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,ERMSG1       ;[DE] := 'Cannot open that file'")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,09H           ;string output function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET                     ;error return")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@READ:   LD      A,2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    GETARG          ;get 2nd argument of command line")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    STOHEX          ;HL := value of the argument")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (ADRS),HL       ;set address counter")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,NEWDMA")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,1AH           ;set DMA address function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (FCB+14),HL     ;record size := 8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,0")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (FCB+33),HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (FCB+35),HL     ;random record := 0")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@RD1:    LD      HL,NEWDMA       ;clear DMA area")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      B,8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@RD2:    LD      (HL)," + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        INC     HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        DJNZ    RD2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,1            ;read 1 record")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,FCB")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,27H           ;random block read function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        OR      A               ;success ?")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      Z,DUMP          ;if so, goto DUMP")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,ERMSG2       ;[DE] := 'Ok.'")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      C,09H           ;string output function")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    BDOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        RET")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@DUMP:   LD      HL,(ADRS)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,H")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A,L")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTHEX")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      A," + Chr(34) + " " + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    PUTCHR")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      DE,8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        ADD     HL,DE")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      (ADRS),HL")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        LD      HL,NEWDMA")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        CALL    DUMP8B          ;dump 8 bytes")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        JR      RD1")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- work area -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ADRS:   DS      2")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@NEWDMA: DS      8")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@;----- error message -----")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ERMSG1: DB      " + Chr(34) + "Cannot open that file.$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@ERMSG2: DB      " + Chr(34) + "Ok.$" + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@        END")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("@@@")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("_List 3.5  File dump_", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#_list-35-file-dump_", 314)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Remarks (1) and (2) about [[[the FCB format|||Chapter3#figure-319--organization-of-fcb]]] in version 2 of MSX-DOS have been added.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- Description of [[[function call 06H|||Chapter3#direct-console-inputoutput]]] is modified. The name of this function in the original text is " + Chr(34) + "String output" + Chr(34) + ", and the setup description is " + Chr(34) + "E register ⟵ starting address of string to send to the console. When 0FF is specified, the character will be sent to the console as character code." + Chr(34))
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- In description of function calls [[[13H (Deleting files)|||Chapter3#deleting-files]]] and [[[23H (File size acquisition)|||Chapter3#file-size-acquisition]]], the original text has " + Chr(34) + "DE register ⟵ starting address of opened FCB" + Chr(34) + " in setup field. Instead of this, the correct setup explanation is set.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- In description of function [[[26H (Random writing to the disk 2)|||Chapter3#random-writing-to-the-disk---2-random-block-access]]], the correct term " + Chr(34) + "FCB" + Chr(34) + " is set instead of " + Chr(34) + "DMA" + Chr(34) + " in DE register setup explanation.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- In description of function [[[27H (Random readout 2)|||Chapter3#random-readout---2-random-block-access]]], the phrase " + Chr(34) + "When this number is almost one, the data which has been read is set in the area indicated by DMA" + Chr(34) + " has been added.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("- In [[[1.2 MSX-DOS Environment|||Chapter3#12-msx-dos-environment]]], " + Chr(34) + "see Chapter 3" + Chr(34) + " has been corrected to " + Chr(34) + "see Section 3" + Chr(34) + ".")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("Changes from the original", "Chapter 3: MSX-DOS")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter3#changes-from-the-original", 315)

  Th2HandbookHelp_Begin()
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("The MSX2 machines uses an advanced VDP (video display processor) for its display screen, the V9938 (MSX-VIDEO). This LSI chip allows for several new graphics features to be accessed by the MSX2 video display. It is also fully compatible with the TMS9918A used in the MSX1.")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("")
  Th2HandbookHelp_L("Chapter 4 describes how to use this video display processor. It describes functions not accessible by BASIC. For mode details (e.g. hardware specifications), see V9938 MSX-VIDEO Technical Data Book (ASCII).")
  Th2HandbookHelp_Commit("CHAPTER 4 - VDP AND DISPLAY SCREEN (Sections 1 to 5)", "Chapter 4: VDP and display screen (1-5)")
  Th2HandbookHelp_AddAnchor("Chapter4a#chapter-4---vdp-and-display-screen-sections-1-to-5", 316)

